To feed URLs into Puppeteer, you use tools that specialize in reconnaissance, subdomain enumeration, directory brute-forcing, and historical web archiving. These tools find the targets, and you pipe them into Puppeteer to render, screenshot, crawl, or audit them. [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] 
Here are all the best bug bounty tools that generate URLs, along with the exact terminal commands to hand them off to Puppeteer.
------------------------------
## 1. Subdomain Enumeration Tools
These tools discover valid subdomains for a target organization. You pass the alive subdomains to Puppeteer to verify what is actually running on them.

* Subfinder & HTTPX (ProjectDiscovery)
* What they do: subfinder finds subdomains; httpx filters out the dead ones and outputs clean HTTP/HTTPS URLs.
   * How to give to Puppeteer:
   
   subfinder -d target.com -silent | httpx -silent > alive_urls.txt
   
   [6, 7] 
* Amass (OWASP)
* What it does: Uses heavy asset mapping and DNS scraping to find hidden subdomains.
   * How to give to Puppeteer:
   
   amass enum -d target.com -o raw_subdomains.txt
   cat raw_subdomains.txt | httpx -silent > alive_urls.txt
   
   
------------------------------
## 2. Historical & Archive Scrapers
These tools crawl third-party internet archives to find old, forgotten endpoints, parameters, and parameters that might still be active. [8] 

* Waybackurls & Gau (GetAllUrls)
* What they do: Fetch thousands of historical URLs belonging to a target from the Wayback Machine, AlienVault, and Common Crawl.
   * How to give to Puppeteer:
   
   # Using gau
   gau target.com --threads 10 | grep -E "\.js$|\?|\=" > archive_urls.txt
   # Using waybackurls
   waybackurls target.com | sort -u > archive_urls.txt
   
   [9, 10, 11] 
* Katana (ProjectDiscovery)
* What it does: A fast, specialized web crawler that automatically outputs clean URL endpoints.
   * How to give to Puppeteer:
   
   katana -u https://target.com -silent -o crawled_urls.txt
   
   [12, 13] 

------------------------------
## 3. Directory & Parameter Fuzzers
These tools brute-force paths to find hidden panels, configuration files, and input fields.

* FFUF (Fuzz Faster U Fool)
* What it does: Discovers hidden directories (like /admin, /backup.zip, /api/v2).
   * How to give to Puppeteer: You can use FFUF's CSV or JSON output to extract valid, successful paths (HTTP 200/302).
   
   ffuf -w wordlist.txt -u https://target.com -of csv -o ffuf_results.csv
   awk -F, 'NR>1 {print $1}' ffuf_results.csv > fuzzed_urls.txt
   
   
------------------------------
## How to Pipe Any of These Lists into Puppeteer
Once you have generated your urls.txt using any tool combo above, you need a Puppeteer script that reads the file line-by-line and processes them.
Create a file named runner.js:

import puppeteer from 'puppeteer';import fs from 'fs';
async function processUrls() {
  // Read the URLs generated by your recon tools
  const urls = fs.readFileSync('urls.txt', 'utf-8').split('\n').filter(Boolean);

  const browser = await puppeteer.launch({
    executablePath: '/usr/bin/chromium-browser',
    args: ['--no-sandbox', '--disable-setuid-sandbox']
  });
  const page = await browser.newPage();

  console.log(`[+] Loaded ${urls.length} URLs for processing.`);

  for (const url of urls) {
    try {
      console.log(`[Scanning]: ${url}`);
      
      // Visit the URL generated by the recon tool
      await page.goto(url, { waitUntil: 'networkidle2', timeout: 10000 });

      // Action 1: Capture a screenshot for visual inspection
      const filename = url.replace(/[^a-z0-9]/gi, '_').toLowerCase();
      await page.screenshot({ path: `./screenshots/${filename}.png` });

      // Action 2: You can also inject XSS payloads, extract DOM metadata, etc.
      
    } catch (err) {
      console.log(`[-] Failed to load ${url}: ${err.message}`);
    }
  }

  await browser.close();
  console.log('[+] Automation pipeline complete.');
}

processUrls();

## The Ultimate One-Liner Automation Chain
You can chain everything into a single line in your Ubuntu terminal. This command finds subdomains, filters live URLs, saves them, and boots your Puppeteer script instantly: [14, 15] 

mkdir -p screenshots && subfinder -d target.com -silent | httpx -silent > urls.txt && node runner.js

